Date: Thu, 18 Feb 1993 23:26:47 -0800
From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: POLI: Reign of Terror?

I mused
> With Operation Sun Devil and the Scott murder, we may be seeing the
> beginning of a genuine Reign of Terror -- a program designed to keep the
> people, "guilty" and "innocent" alike, too scared to oppose the state. 

and Scott Meeks fretted
> "I'm familiar with Sun Devil but what's the story on the Scott murder?" he
> asks worriedly.

and Krzys' confessed
> I am ignorant. Please illuminate me -- what are those?

These stories are nth-hand; others may tell them better.

About three years ago, the Secret Service (looking for a way to make
themselves important, perhaps) got wind that some crackers (phreakers,
sneakers?) had downloaded a document about the 911 system from a BellSouth
computer.  They freaked, figuring that the kids planned to use the
information to disrupt 911 service, and in "Operation Sun Devil" raided
anyone within three e-mail links of the crackers -- seizing dozens of
computers and printers, and thousands of disks.  Steve Jackson Games, one of
whose employees was indirectly connected to the "gang", was almost put out
of business by the seizure of its work in progress.  Prosecution collapsed
when a BellSouth employee admitted that the document in question (a
glossary, I'm told) was available to anyone for about $13.  Steve Jackson
Games sued the feds; a judge recently berated the defendant (I think that
was reported here).

Los Angeles police raided the Ventura County home of the heir to the Scott
paper fortune, looking for marijuana so they could seize the place.  Scott
heard burglars, got out of bed with his gun, and was killed.  (I call it
"murder", though others might not, because by any justice I would recognize
Scott was clearly killed in the course of a felony.)

