Tim, could you please stick to one subject heading?  Our thread on Coase
and justication keeps appearing under different headings, yet the subject
has not noticably changed.


>But, Tom, this doesn't follow the lines of your original claim.  You had
>to show that you had an overriding right to smoke - she didn't have to show
>that you didn't.

Huh?  I just do not understand this.  I was not smoking in the example;
"she" was.

>I don't see why I couldn't expressly consent to being ruled by any and all
>that might wish to do so.  Why wouldn't this be expressly consenting to
>statism in general?  Why can't abstact doctrines be expressly consented to?

But even then, you are not consenting to a doctrine.  You are consenting to
its application to your person.  Can you, in the Bay Area, consent to
statism in 5th century Armenia?  No.  It has no impact on you, so there is
no place for your consent to "take hold."  Similarly, I'm arguing that you
cannot (not) consent to statism as an abstract doctrine.  You can only give
your nay or yea to its particular application.    

Put it another way:  How do you withhold consent from an abstract doctrine?
 So long as it is still mere theory, there is no need for you to kick and
squirm.  That is not to say that you cannot object to it on similarly
abstract grounds.  But until someone tries to apply the doctrine to you,
there is no need to test your consent.  Consent is a measure of how you
will dispose of your person and property.  Because pure theory cannot
affect these goods, there is not place for consent to come into question.


